WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.185
Embedded C security means protecting the
firmware, device behavior,

2
00:00:04.265 --> 00:00:06.700
external interfaces, secrets, update
paths,

3
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.029
and operational reliability of a product.

4
00:00:09.109 --> 00:00:13.231
It is not limited to one library, one
compiler option, or one hardware feature.

5
00:00:13.311 --> 00:00:17.058
It is the discipline of making sure the
code that runs on a device behaves

6
00:00:17.138 --> 00:00:20.635
predictably under the conditions the
product will actually face.

7
00:00:20.715 --> 00:00:23.151
Embedded devices often run close to
hardware.

8
00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:26.478
Firmware may read sensors, drive
actuators, manage storage,

9
00:00:26.558 --> 00:00:30.555
handle radios or field buses, process
commands, maintain device identity,

10
00:00:30.635 --> 00:00:32.509
or coordinate with a cloud service.

11
00:00:32.589 --> 00:00:35.961
In that setting, a software defect can
affect confidentiality,

12
00:00:36.041 --> 00:00:39.851
integrity, availability, safety
assumptions, field serviceability,

13
00:00:39.931 --> 00:00:42.054
and the trust users place in the product.

14
00:00:42.134 --> 00:00:46.256
C is powerful in embedded work because it
gives developers direct control over

15
00:00:46.336 --> 00:00:50.458
memory, timing, registers, interrupt
paths, and low-level device behavior.

16
00:00:50.538 --> 00:00:54.598
That control also means the code must be
clear about bounds, initialization,

17
00:00:54.678 --> 00:00:57.863
pointer lifetime, ownership, cleanup, and
error handling.

18
00:00:57.943 --> 00:01:02.127
The language will not automatically
protect every memory access or every state

19
00:01:02.207 --> 00:01:02.894
transition.

20
00:01:02.974 --> 00:01:06.909
Embedded software also has a different
lifecycle from many applications.

21
00:01:06.989 --> 00:01:10.673
A device may remain in service for years,
patch windows may be limited,

22
00:01:10.753 --> 00:01:13.876
and physical replacement may be expensive
or impractical.

23
00:01:13.956 --> 00:01:17.766
Design choices about update support,
diagnostics, secret management,

24
00:01:17.846 --> 00:01:22.093
and recovery behavior can determine
whether the product remains maintainable.

25
00:01:22.173 --> 00:01:26.107
Secure embedded design begins by treating
firmware, inputs, interfaces,

26
00:01:26.187 --> 00:01:29.747
update mechanisms, and stored state as
security-critical assets.

27
00:01:29.827 --> 00:01:33.824
Developers should ask what the device
trusts, what can influence behavior,

28
00:01:33.904 --> 00:01:35.840
what happens when validation fails,

29
00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.793
and how field teams will diagnose issues
responsibly.

30
00:01:38.873 --> 00:01:41.808
The purpose of this course is to build
practical habits.

31
00:01:41.888 --> 00:01:45.136
Define trust boundaries, keep memory
behavior predictable,

32
00:01:45.216 --> 00:01:47.526
validate external input, protect secrets,

33
00:01:47.606 --> 00:01:50.354
rely on platform-supported integrity
mechanisms,

34
00:01:50.434 --> 00:01:52.495
control debug and maintenance access,

35
00:01:52.575 --> 00:01:56.260
and test both success and failure paths
before the device is deployed.
